# Line endings, pinned so that a clone is byte-identical to what was committed.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `git init` on this machine set core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. That produced a CRLF warning for well over a hundred files at the first
# `git add`, and left alone it would mean (a) every collaborator's checkout differs from the commit,
# and (b) the first edit by anyone on Unix shows the whole file as changed. Pinning the endings in
# the repository rather than in one machine's config is the fix: the setting travels with the code.
#
# The paper is the reason this matters more than usual here. `paper/main.tex`, the generated tables
# and the CSVs are all inputs to a build whose numbers are checked mechanically, and silent
# whitespace rewriting is exactly the kind of change that is invisible in a diff and visible in a
# checksum.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, so the intent survives a change to the line above.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.tex   text eol=lf
*.bib   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf

# Binary. Declared so that Git never attempts an ending conversion or a text diff on them; the
# figures and the compiled manuscript are deliverables, not sources, but they are committed so a
# reader can see the artifact the numbers produce without installing a TeX toolchain.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.zip   binary

# Generated LaTeX from matplotlib (fig_method.tex) is a source input to the build, so it is text.
*.eps   binary
